<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Colors in My Classroom – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors in My Classroom</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Colors in My Classroom</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow very simple classroom instructions after observing a model. Interactive: Engage in acting out and response activities as a way of interaction.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow simple classroom instructions like "sit" and "stand" (e.g., sitting when hearing the command "sit"). Can point to objects when named (e.g., pointing to the object or picture of a chair when hearing the word "chair"). Can act out and respond to familiar vocabulary used in daily routines (e.g., "find a pencil").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a "Hello Song" using actions. Then, the teacher plays a recording of a school bell ringing, and asks students to listen for the sound. The teacher introduces a "Colors in My Classroom" song (e.g., "The Red Crayon") with simple actions (pointing to objects or colors around the room). Students listen and mimic actions, starting to associate colors like "red," "blue," and "yellow" with classroom objects. The teacher holds up realia (a red pencil, a blue book) and says the words clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a pencil and says, "pencil." Then, the teacher says "red pencil" and points to the color. The teacher models the pronunciation of /p/ in "pencil" and /b/ in "book" clearly and slowly. The teacher gives simple instructions like "sit down" and "stand up," performing the actions themselves, and inviting students to copy using TPR. For "find a crayon," the teacher models picking up a crayon. For "The book is on the table," the teacher places a book on the table.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a pencil) "Is this a crayon or a pencil?" (Teacher points to a crayon, then a pencil, eliciting pointing response from students).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a red object) "Is this blue?" (Teacher shakes head for "no" and points to a blue object, then red object to get a nod or pointing to the correct color).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors in My Classroom."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short animated picture story or video about "My Colorful Classroom" showing a child finding different colored objects (pencil, book, chair, desk, crayon, board, table, bag). The story includes simple sentences like "Look! A red pencil," "The blue book is on the table." It also incorporates sounds of chairs moving and voices greeting. The story is presented with clear audio and engaging visuals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying classroom objects and colors: Responding to "What is this?" by naming familiar items (e.g., "pencil") and "What color is this?" by naming familiar colors (e.g., "red"). Following directions: Pointing to or retrieving items based on instructions (e.g., "Pick up the book.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (Listening & Pointing):</b> The teacher pauses the video/story at key moments and asks, "Where is the red pencil?" or "What color is the book?" Students listen and point to the correct object/color on the screen or on flashcards provided.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Visual Check):</b> The teacher makes a statement about a visual from the story (e.g., "The chair is yellow.") while showing the image. Students indicate "true" by giving a thumbs up or "false" by thumbs down, based on what they heard and saw.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Sound to Object):</b> The teacher plays a sound from the story (e.g., a chair moving) and asks, "What is this sound?" Students listen and point to the picture of a chair.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening for specific vocabulary (pencil, book, chair, desk, crayon, board, table, bag, red, blue, yellow) and simple instructions (sit, stand, find, point to, on, under, in). They will also practice receptive understanding of "What color is it?" and "Is this your pencil?". Pronunciation focus on /t/ in "chair" and /f/ in "find."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Listen and Do" (TPR Game):</b> The teacher gives commands using classroom objects and colors, emphasizing the initial sounds /t/ in "chair" and /f/ in "find." Students listen and perform the action.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Sit on your chair!" (Teacher models, students listen and sit).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Stand up!" (Teacher models, students listen and stand).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the red crayon!" (Teacher points, students listen and point).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Find a blue book!" (Teacher models finding, students listen and find/point).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this your pencil?" (Teacher holds up a pencil, students nod/shake head).</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Listen and Locate" (Preposition Focus):</b> The teacher uses flashcards or realia of classroom objects and asks students to listen to instructions involving prepositions (on, under, in, next to). This also includes the environmental sound of drawers opening.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Put the book on the table." (Teacher models, students listen and put a toy book on a toy table).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Put the crayon in the bag." (Teacher models, students listen and place a toy crayon in a toy bag).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The pencil is under the desk." (Teacher places a pencil under a toy desk. Students listen to the sentence and point to the correct location).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "Listen! (Plays sound of drawers opening). Find something in the drawer!" (Students listen and retrieve an object from a box/drawer).</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Teacher-Guided):</b> Students work in pairs with classroom object flashcards. One student (or the teacher) gives a simple instruction, and the other student listens and points. The teacher circulates, observing and assisting.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A (or Teacher): "Point to the yellow crayon."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: (Listens and points to the yellow crayon flashcard).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "What color is it?" (Students point to red or blue color card).</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors in My Classroom."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Classroom Treasure Hunt (Listening Edition)". This is a guided group activity where students work collaboratively to listen to and follow multi-step instructions from the teacher to find specific classroom objects of certain colors.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher hides 3-4 colored classroom objects (e.g., a red pencil, a blue book, a yellow crayon) in visible but not obvious spots around the classroom.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives a series of two-step listening instructions to the whole group or small groups. For example: "Go to the table. Find the red pencil!" or "Look under the chair. Find the blue book!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the full instruction, process it, and then act out the steps (going to the table, looking, finding the object).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When a student finds an object, the teacher reinforces by saying, "You found the red pencil! Very good!" The teacher also prompts, "Say 'thank you'!" if another student helps or gives them the object.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The activity continues until all objects are found, encouraging active listening and physical response.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> This activity focuses on students' ability to listen to sequential instructions, identify objects and colors, and follow directions using prepositions. It promotes collaborative interaction as students might look together or point for each other. Fluency in listening and responding with actions is key.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher will observe students during the "Classroom Treasure Hunt" and a final individual listening task to assess their ability to follow instructions and identify vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Individual "Listen and Point" Challenge. The teacher calls students individually or in very small groups. The teacher places 3-4 familiar classroom objects (e.g., pencil, book, crayon, chair flashcard) on a table along with color cards (red, blue, yellow). The teacher gives 3-4 simple commands or questions, and the student listens and points to the correct item or performs the action.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the blue crayon."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Sit down." (Student sits).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a book?" (Teacher holds up a book, student nods).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What color is this?" (Teacher points to a red object, student points to the red color card).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can follow simple instructions like "sit," "stand," "point to."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can point to target classroom objects (pencil, book, chair, crayon).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can point to target colors (red, blue, yellow) when named or asked.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows engagement and willingness to respond.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are shown two large emoji faces (happy and confused/neutral). After the activities, the teacher asks, "Did you understand the colors and words today?" Students point to the happy face if they understood well, or the neutral face if they need more help. The teacher can also ask, "Can you find the red crayon?" and students listen and point to a red crayon in the room.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite color we learned today?" (Students point to a color or color card).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you hear the /p/ sound in 'pencil'?" (Teacher says "pencil" slowly, students nod if they hear it).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., TPR activities, using realia, the treasure hunt).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face? (e.g., following multi-step instructions, differentiating specific sounds).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., more visual cues, slower pronunciation, repeating instructions).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students engage with the pronunciation and environmental sounds effectively?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Great listening today, everyone! Next time, we will see these colorful classroom objects in a special book and learn more words. Get ready to look at pictures and hear new stories!" (Setting the stage for Reading Comprehension).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>